{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1360 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cac0688130ebead071574331ca5b611abba353a9a864846ab245699db7e2b332" :edb/db-before-t 1359 :edb/db-after-t 1360 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534672 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:50:32.634Z" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087450 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/6d2ba3fba1a05a841a486553ea3a52d6f3797974630420ad79ae50f4c6f5bc15.png)" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087450 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/e5e3e1baad54a5ce65047fbcb5e7a58d.png)" 13194139534672 false] [17592186087450 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{5^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534672 false] [17592186087450 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087451 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/3e8d5ed4bf055960b50e046c0688a3c4cc69907ebb1209bb9658ac4bcba48ffe.png)" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087451 1100 "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/10eddd924b3dc68fbf1bb4eaeb8f92c5.png)" 13194139534672 false] [17592186087451 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{2^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534672 false] [17592186087451 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087452 1100 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1$?" 13194139534672 false] [17592186087452 1100 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1?$" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087452 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25$.\n\n- Since $a^2=36$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6$.\n- Since $b^2=25$, the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6$, and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5$.\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length\n\n$$\n2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12\n$$\n\nand the minor axis has length\n\n$$\n2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10\n$$" 13194139534672 false] [17592186087452 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25.$\n\n- Since $a^2=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6.$\n\n- Since $b^2=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10.$" 13194139534672 true] [17592186087778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302602 13194139534672 true] [17592186087778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302606 13194139534672 true] [17592186087779 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302588 13194139534672 true] [17592186087779 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302620 13194139534672 true] [17592186087779 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302627 13194139534672 true] [17592186087779 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302648 13194139534672 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302595 13194139534672 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302609 13194139534672 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302614 13194139534672 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302634 13194139534672 true] [17592186087780 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302641 13194139534672 true] [17592186088267 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534672 true] [17592186088267 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/8ac809353e7834d328d645fafb5c02e17386ba087e778a841865ef6313893e79.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 true] [17592186088267 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/q-7603.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 false] [17592186088267 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{2}^{2}})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{49}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534672 true] [17592186088268 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/d2c35e72acad77ae747c6a78c9794e9b076c047891d4b12d5c9b3176bdc6dde5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 true] [17592186088268 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/q-7604.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 false] [17592186267445 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/6116ecf00a26d9828b238879b61385c1296f33ff863dbb8337a3b22be5981f10.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 true] [17592186267445 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13957964/assets/6116ecf00a26d9828b238879b61385c1296f33ff863dbb8337a3b22be5981f10.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 false] [17592186267491 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/8637901efc126236b190e7012cf65a06be078cb21c3093eff39b927493000138.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 true] [17592186267491 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13957964/assets/8637901efc126236b190e7012cf65a06be078cb21c3093eff39b927493000138.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 false] [17592186302588 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302589 13194139534672 true] [17592186302588 1089 "q-115129" 13194139534672 true] [17592186302588 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534672 true] [17592186302588 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/c71a52cc148f8ce4a23a2fb2005757c9d141d3caf05586d8fb5122f591dc7474.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139534672 true] [17592186302588 1113 #uuid "a6b7394b-01c9-52bb-809f-2cab825b18da" 13194139534672 true] [17592186302588 1145 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534672 true] [17592186302589 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302590 1